The static review found no missing used construct and no false ordinary small-step rule on the intended executions. The critical rule is the priority-20 whole-program bridge: it exactly duplicates the target conclusion, skips every assignment and loop, has no bridge-free universal connection theorem, and changes observable final state. I’m now running a separate off-by-one postcondition mutation to confirm that, despite this unsound shortcut, the claim still constrains the result rather than closing vacuously.
